lithfetch: system information for the LispBSD X1 Nano, printed the way a Lisp
machine printed its herald at boot: system and boot file, machine, the
software loaded, one line per fact, and a status line at the bottom.  The λ
is the LispBSD logo from our pfetch.

  lithfetch            the herald
  lithfetch --sexp     everything it knows, as a Lisp property list
  lithfetch --help

It is meant to be changed.  ~/.config/lithfetch/init.lisp is LOADed raw into
the LITHFETCH package after the defaults (interpreted, warnings muffled, an
error is reported and the run goes on), so it can redefine anything:
DEFINE-FACT, DEFINE-ENTRY, *HERALD* (the order), *PALETTE* (roles to
cl-colorist styles), *LOGO*, *STATUS*, or any function.  See
init.lisp.example; try a file with lithfetch --init FILE.

Source: src/facts.lisp (what it learns: sysctl, envstat, ifconfig, vmstat,
df, mixerctl, dmesg.boot, and the running Mahogany through mahoganyctl),
src/herald.lisp (the lines), src/look.lisp (palette, bars), src/logo.lisp.
Only dependency: cl-colorist (lambda-symbolics), from cclsh's sources.
Build on the laptop as root: ./build.sh -> /usr/local/bin/lithfetch (an SBCL
executable, ~80 ms per run).  Sources live in /usr/local/lib/lithfetch.
